1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:00,900
What you're looking at

2
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,470
is a chain of circles.

3
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:04,240
Each one rides on the edge

4
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,510
of the one before,

5
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:07,710
spinning at its own steady speed.

6
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:10,460
Follow the tip of the very last

7
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,740
circle, and it draws a treble clef.

8
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:15,670
Here's what I find strange about this.

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,260
A circle is about the

10
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:18,490
roundest thing there is.

11
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:21,020
No corners, no straight edges.

12
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:22,140
And yet the right circles

13
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:23,540
can draw sharp turns,

14
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:26,140
even a square with four perfect corners.

15
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:27,940
So how does something that

16
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:30,030
only goes round draw a corner?

17
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:33,220
The answer is something called a Fourier

18
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:35,340
series, named after Joseph Fourier,

19
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,260
who found it about two hundred years ago

20
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:39,520
while studying, of all things, heat.

21
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,430
And it makes a bold claim.

22
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,600
Almost any loop you could draw can

23
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,510
be built out of spinning circles.

24
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:47,930
Think of a chord on a piano.

25
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:49,980
You hear one sound, but it's really

26
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:51,550
a few notes played together.

27
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,200
A Fourier series lets you take a shape

28
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:56,000
and pick out the circles inside it.

29
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:58,060
By the end, you and I will

30
00:00:58,060 --> 00:00:59,440
have a recipe that finds the

31
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:00,890
circles for any drawing.

32
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,460
I'll put it on screen now,

33
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:03,900
just so you'll recognize it later.

34
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:05,840
As with anything new,

35
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,210
it's best to start easy.

36
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,580
So let's begin with a single circle.

37
00:01:10,290 --> 00:01:11,520
Picture an arrow pinned at

38
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:13,220
the center, turning steadily.

39
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:15,160
For now, ignore everything

40
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:16,960
except how high its tip is.

41
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:17,820
Up,

42
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:19,090
down, up,

43
00:01:19,290 --> 00:01:19,730
down.

44
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:22,150
Now let time move to the right,

45
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:24,080
and let that height leave a trail.

46
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,580
What you get is a sine wave.

47
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:28,100
It's the simplest wave there

48
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,040
is, and really, it's just a

49
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:31,220
circle seen from the side.

50
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:34,210
A spinning circle has only three settings.

51
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:35,460
There's its size.

52
00:01:35,670 --> 00:01:37,620
A bigger circle makes a taller wave.

53
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:39,330
There's its speed.

54
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:40,680
Spin twice as fast,

55
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,570
and the wave wiggles twice as often.

56
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:44,810
And there's where it starts.

57
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:47,080
Start the arrow a quarter turn ahead,

58
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,940
and the whole wave slides over.

59
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:51,760
Size, speed, and starting point.

60
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:53,950
That's everything one circle can do.

61
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:56,200
There's another view I really like.

62
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,460
Slide the circle forward through time,

63
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:00,550
and its tip traces out a spring.

64
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:02,160
From the side, the spring

65
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:03,280
looks like a sine wave.

66
00:02:03,590 --> 00:02:04,760
From above, it looks like

67
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,290
its twin, the cosine.

68
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:08,870
Two shadows of one turning motion.

69
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:11,990
One circle gives you one smooth wave.

70
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:13,500
So let's add a second.

71
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:15,460
Put a smaller circle on the

72
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:16,340
tip of the first,

73
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,530
and let it spin three times as fast.

74
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:20,600
Now the tip rides on two motions

75
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:22,360
at once, so its height is the

76
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,030
first height plus the second.

77
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:26,610
The wave picks up a little ripple.

78
00:02:27,330 --> 00:02:28,990
Now let's be more deliberate.

79
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:31,440
Make the first circle a little taller

80
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:32,770
than the wave we're aiming for.

81
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:34,960
The second spins three times as

82
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:36,980
fast, at one third the size.

83
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:38,030
The next,

84
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:40,290
five times as fast, at one fifth.

85
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:42,930
Then seven times, at one seventh.

86
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:45,250
Before I keep going,

87
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:46,400
take a moment to guess.

88
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,260
What do you think this is turning into?

89
00:02:49,330 --> 00:02:50,800
Watch the top flatten out,

90
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,250
and the sides get steeper.

91
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:54,290
Here it is with ten circles...

92
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:55,370
fifty...

93
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:56,850
two hundred.

94
00:02:57,780 --> 00:02:58,900
It's a square wave.

95
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:01,240
Flat on top, flat on the bottom,

96
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:02,910
with straight walls in between.

97
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:04,300
Smooth circles,

98
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:05,940
adding up to sharp corners.

99
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,110
But look closely at a corner.

100
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:09,880
Right before every jump,

101
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:11,160
there's a small spike.

102
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,320
Add more circles, and the spike gets

103
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:15,630
thinner, but it never gets any shorter.

104
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:16,885
It stays at about nine

105
00:03:16,885 --> 00:03:17,980
percent of the jump.

106
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:20,560
This is called the Gibbs phenomenon,

107
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:21,790
and it's telling us something.

108
00:03:22,090 --> 00:03:24,170
Sharp corners are expensive.

109
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:26,540
Now you might be wondering why

110
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:27,820
anyone would build waves out of

111
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:29,170
circles in the first place.

112
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:31,180
The short answer is heat.

113
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:33,020
In eighteen oh seven,

114
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:34,500
Fourier was trying to describe

115
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,230
how heat spreads through a solid.

116
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:37,700
Take a metal ring,

117
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:39,380
heat one half with a flame,

118
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:40,580
cool the other half with

119
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:41,970
ice, and let it go.

120
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,840
Hot on one side, cold on the other.

121
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:46,260
Unroll the ring,

122
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:47,730
and that's our square wave.

123
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:50,200
Fourier's move was to break the

124
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:51,960
temperature into simple waves.

125
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,740
One that goes around the ring once,

126
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:55,100
one that goes around three

127
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:57,140
times, then five, and so on.

128
00:03:57,830 --> 00:03:58,920
Each of those waves does

129
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:00,120
something very simple.

130
00:04:00,370 --> 00:04:02,850
It keeps its shape, and it just fades.

131
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:04,310
And the wigglier the wave,

132
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:05,560
the faster it fades.

133
00:04:05,890 --> 00:04:07,120
Three times the wiggles,

134
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,460
nine times as fast.

135
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,350
So the sharp edges melt away first,

136
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:13,720
the ring settles into one gentle wave,

137
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,760
and then into an even warmth.

138
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:17,730
But for this to work,

139
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:19,080
any starting temperature,

140
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:20,700
even one with sudden jumps,

141
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:23,050
had to be a sum of smooth waves.

142
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:24,520
Some of the best mathematicians

143
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:26,100
of his day didn't buy it.

144
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:28,950
Well, you and I just watched it happen.

145
00:04:29,750 --> 00:04:30,980
Now let's bring all this

146
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:32,000
back to our drawing.

147
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:33,940
So far, we've only watched

148
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,220
the height of the tip.

149
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:36,990
But it also moves left and right.

150
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:38,400
So let's use both,

151
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,460
and let the tip draw

152
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:40,440
right on the page.

153
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:43,360
This picture is actually very old.

154
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:45,420
Nearly two thousand years ago,

155
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:46,820
astronomers tracked the planets

156
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:48,970
with circles riding on circles.

157
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:51,160
They were wrong about how the sky works.

158
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:53,130
But they were right about the circles.

159
00:04:53,130 --> 00:04:54,250
With enough of them,

160
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:56,130
you can follow almost any path.

161
00:04:56,790 --> 00:04:59,090
On a page, direction matters.

162
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:01,380
A circle can turn counterclockwise,

163
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:02,960
which we'll call a positive speed,

164
00:05:03,230 --> 00:05:05,090
or clockwise, a negative speed.

165
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:07,140
A circle with speed zero

166
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:08,210
doesn't turn at all.

167
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:10,170
It just sets where the drawing sits.

168
00:05:11,370 --> 00:05:12,500
Here's a friendly example

169
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:13,740
we'll keep coming back to.

170
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:16,730
Take a big circle turning forward once,

171
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:18,050
and add a small one,

172
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:19,380
one ninth the size,

173
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,540
turning backward three times as fast.

174
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,180
The tip traces out a rounded square.

175
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:27,290
And the square from the beginning?

176
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,200
Its first two circles are exactly these.

177
00:05:31,230 --> 00:05:32,820
In general, we line up circles

178
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:34,410
of every whole number speed.

179
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:36,760
Zero, one, minus one,

180
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:38,630
two, minus two, and so on.

181
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,120
Each has two settings left to choose,

182
00:05:41,300 --> 00:05:43,180
its size and its starting angle.

183
00:05:44,230 --> 00:05:45,620
So here's the real question.

184
00:05:45,910 --> 00:05:47,380
If I hand you a drawing,

185
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,080
how do you find the size and

186
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:50,760
starting angle of every circle?

187
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:53,720
Let's warm up with the easiest circle,

188
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:54,850
speed zero.

189
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:57,560
It never moves, so it should sit

190
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:58,880
at the center of the drawing.

191
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:00,900
But what does center even mean,

192
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,470
for a squiggly shape like this?

193
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:04,580
Here's a natural answer.

194
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:06,660
Spread dots along the path,

195
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:08,360
evenly in time, and average

196
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:09,380
all their positions.

197
00:06:09,790 --> 00:06:11,480
That average is the balance point

198
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:13,200
of the drawing, and it's exactly

199
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,010
where the speed zero circle sits.

200
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:17,640
That's because every other circle

201
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:19,130
spins all the way around,

202
00:06:19,130 --> 00:06:21,450
and a full turn averages out to nothing.

203
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:24,190
That gives us the key idea.

204
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,820
Things that spin average out to zero.

205
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:28,870
Things that stand still don't.

206
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:32,000
Now, the speed one circle spins,

207
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,330
so the average can't see it.

208
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:35,620
Unless we can make it stand still.

209
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:37,540
Here's the trick.

210
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:40,000
As the pen goes around, turn the whole

211
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,530
drawing backward, one full turn per loop.

212
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,600
The speed one circle was turning

213
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:46,720
forward once, and now it's also

214
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:48,750
turned backward once, so it stops.

215
00:06:49,650 --> 00:06:51,630
Every other circle is still spinning,

216
00:06:51,630 --> 00:06:53,680
so when we average, they all wash out.

217
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:55,800
What's left is the speed one

218
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:57,500
circle, frozen in place.

219
00:06:58,470 --> 00:07:00,580
As a sanity check, let's try our rounded

220
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:02,110
square, where we know the answer.

221
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:04,360
Turn it backward once and average,

222
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:05,800
and the balance point lands right

223
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,350
on the tip of the big circle.

224
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:09,590
Turn it forward three times,

225
00:07:09,590 --> 00:07:11,060
and it finds the small circle,

226
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:13,110
one ninth the size, just as it should.

227
00:07:13,910 --> 00:07:15,120
Try speed two,

228
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:16,820
and the average falls to zero.

229
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:18,260
There's no circle there.

230
00:07:19,170 --> 00:07:20,920
If you hold on to just one idea

231
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:22,860
from this video, make it this one.

232
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,720
To find any circle, spin the drawing

233
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:27,430
against it, and take the average.

234
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:28,800
Now we can read the line

235
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:29,680
from the beginning.

236
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:31,500
C n equals the

237
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:32,380
integral from zero

238
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:34,260
to one, of f of t,

239
00:07:34,260 --> 00:07:35,480
times e to the minus

240
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:37,864
two pi i n t, d t.

241
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:39,240
It looks dense,

242
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:41,110
but it's really just two steps.

243
00:07:41,570 --> 00:07:42,900
F of t is the pen's

244
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:44,210
position at time t.

245
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:46,460
Multiplying by e to the minus

246
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:48,960
two pi i n t turns the drawing

247
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:50,780
backward, n times per loop.

248
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:52,660
If you saw our video on complex

249
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:54,180
numbers, that's just a point

250
00:07:54,180 --> 00:07:55,300
turning around a circle.

251
00:07:55,730 --> 00:07:57,820
Then the integral from zero to one

252
00:07:57,820 --> 00:08:00,050
takes the average over one trip around.

253
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:02,100
Spin, then average.

254
00:08:02,270 --> 00:08:03,370
That's all it says.

255
00:08:03,760 --> 00:08:05,370
So let's give it a real test.

256
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:06,820
Here's our treble clef,

257
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:08,440
as one continuous line.

258
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,340
We place a thousand dots along

259
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,980
it, and for every speed from

260
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:13,174
minus one hundred to one

261
00:08:13,174 --> 00:08:15,200
hundred, we spin and average.

262
00:08:15,630 --> 00:08:17,810
Out come two hundred and one circles.

263
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:19,920
Sort them from biggest to smallest,

264
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:21,480
and chain them tip to tail.

265
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,770
With one circle, we get a loop.

266
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:25,680
With five, a rough blob.

267
00:08:26,070 --> 00:08:27,990
By twenty, you can start to see it.

268
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:29,540
And with two hundred,

269
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:30,600
the circles trace the

270
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:32,240
clef almost perfectly.

271
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:34,840
Up close, even the tiniest circles

272
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:36,770
are adding detail the others can't.

273
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,460
Remember how I said sharp

274
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:39,670
corners are expensive?

275
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:41,160
Smooth curves come from

276
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:42,530
the first few circles.

277
00:08:42,750 --> 00:08:44,730
Sharp turns need all the rest.

278
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:46,900
When the pen jumps, like in the square

279
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:48,970
wave, the circles shrink slowly.

280
00:08:49,190 --> 00:08:49,980
One third,

281
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:50,850
one fifth.

282
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:52,960
When the pen only turns a corner,

283
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,380
like our square, they shrink much faster.

284
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,361
One ninth, one twenty fifth.

285
00:08:57,760 --> 00:08:58,990
The smoother the shape,

286
00:08:58,990 --> 00:09:00,590
the fewer circles it takes.

287
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,300
That's why this idea shows up everywhere.

288
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,060
Think back to the piano chord.

289
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:06,770
A sound is a wave,

290
00:09:06,770 --> 00:09:08,100
so it has circles too.

291
00:09:08,270 --> 00:09:10,040
A singer and a violin playing

292
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,960
the same note spin their slowest

293
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,640
circle at the same speed.

294
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:15,500
It's the faster circles on top

295
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:16,720
that make them sound different.

296
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:18,820
And your phone uses a cousin of this

297
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:20,980
idea to shrink photos and music,

298
00:09:20,980 --> 00:09:22,640
keeping the big circles and dropping

299
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:24,330
the tiny ones you won't notice.

300
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:25,820
So let's step back and

301
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:26,920
look at what we've built.

302
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:28,560
One spinning circle gives you

303
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,800
the simplest wave, with a size,

304
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:32,470
a speed, and a starting point.

305
00:09:32,830 --> 00:09:34,420
Stack circles tip to tail,

306
00:09:34,420 --> 00:09:35,950
and their motions add up.

307
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:37,970
With the right sizes and speeds,

308
00:09:37,970 --> 00:09:40,400
smooth circles can build sharp corners.

309
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:43,000
Fourier's insight was that almost any

310
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,800
repeating shape can be built this way.

311
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:47,050
And once the tip moves in two directions,

312
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:49,000
those waves become drawings.

313
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:51,220
To find the circles, you spin the

314
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,250
drawing backward and take the average.

315
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:55,360
Whatever stands still is the

316
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:56,620
circle you were looking for.

317
00:09:57,190 --> 00:09:58,120
There's one thing we

318
00:09:58,120 --> 00:09:59,440
quietly assumed, though.

319
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:01,440
Our drawings repeat.

320
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:03,100
The pen always comes back

321
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:04,050
to where it started.

322
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:06,740
What about a sound that never repeats?

323
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:09,480
Then you need circles of every possible

324
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:11,230
speed, not just whole numbers.

325
00:10:11,610 --> 00:10:13,480
That's the Fourier transform,

326
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:14,840
and it's where we'll go next.

327
00:10:15,260 --> 00:10:17,490
For now, let's watch it one more time.

328
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:19,650
Just circles, going round.

329
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:21,190
Thanks for watching.

